{
  "image_name": "0fef563f566d7989093ef365507669dd.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure presents a brain-circuit model linking opioid use to motor activation, dependence, and relapse. It overlays several interacting pathways—glutamatergic, GABAergic, dopaminergic, and opioid peptide signaling (enkephalin and dynorphin)—across cortical, basal ganglia, limbic, and midbrain regions.\n\nA central idea is that altered input from the prefrontal cortex creates an imbalance in neurotransmission that converges on the nucleus accumbens (NAc). From there, reduced GABAergic control over motor systems appears to disinhibit downstream structures, including the caudate, putamen, substantia nigra, and motor cortical areas. The diagram highlights motor cortex regions 4, 6, and 8, suggesting enhanced extrapyramidal motor output and activation of descending spinal motor pathways.\n\nAt the same time, the ventral tegmental area, amygdala, and hippocampal formation are heavily interconnected, indicating reinforcement and memory-related processes. The hippocampus and dentate gyrus are specifically associated with enhanced plasticity, while the amygdala and related limbic areas seem to integrate emotional and reward signals. Overall, the figure argues that opioid-related changes in reward, memory, and motor circuitry jointly drive the behavioral outcomes shown at right: opioid dependence and relapse, expressed as drug seeking.",
